# Commit identity

The `identity` job in `.github/workflows/tests.yml` runs `harness/check_commit_identity.py`.
It fails any commit not on `origin/main` whose author email, committer email, or any trailer
email (Co-authored-by, Signed-off-by, or another) differs from `ALLOWED` in that script,
currently `73196388+njs14@users.noreply.github.com`.

Cursor Cloud Agents commit as Cursor Agent whatever the repo's git config says, so before every
push, rewrite this branch's own commits:

```sh
git fetch origin main
id=73196388+njs14@users.noreply.github.com
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=njs14 GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=$id GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=njs14 GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=$id \
  git rebase --rebase-merges "$(git merge-base HEAD origin/main)" \
  --exec 'git log -1 --format=%B | sed -E "/^[A-Za-z-]+: .*<[^>]*@[^>]*>[[:space:]]*$/d" | git commit -q --amend --reset-author --cleanup=strip -F -'
python3 harness/check_commit_identity.py
```

- The inline `GIT_AUTHOR_*` and `GIT_COMMITTER_*` variables win over any identity Cursor sets in
  the environment.
- Rebasing onto the merge base rewrites only this branch's commits. It does not move the branch
  onto a newer `main`.
- The `sed` removes every trailer line that carries an email address.
- Push only when the check prints `commit identity ok`. A branch that was already pushed needs
  `git push --force-with-lease`, because the rewrite changes every commit's SHA.
